We introduce Coral, a system for proving in zeroknowledge that a committed byte stream corresponds to a structured object in accordance to a Context Free Grammar. Once a prover establishes the validity of the parsed object with Coral, they can selectively prove facts about the object-such as fields in Web API responses or in JSON Web Tokens-to third parties or blockchains. Coral reduces the problem of correct parsing to a few simple checks over a left-child right-sibling tree and introduces a novel segmented memory abstraction that unifies and extends prior constructions for RAM in zkSNARKs. Our implementation of Coral runs on a standard laptop, and non-interactively proves the parsing of real Web responses (JSON) and files (TOML and C) in seconds. The resulting proofs are small and cheap to verify.